    &lt;/RoutePriceUpdateEvent&gt;
    &lt;CustomerPriceUpdateEvent&gt;
        &lt;weightcost&gt;3.0&lt;/weightcost&gt;
        &lt;to&gt;Wellington&lt;/to&gt;
        &lt;priority&gt;air priority&lt;/priority&gt;
        &lt;from&gt;Sydney&lt;/from&gt;
        &lt;volumecost&gt;2.0&lt;/volumecost&gt;
    &lt;/CustomerPriceUpdateEvent&gt;
&lt;/BusinessEvents&gt;
</code></pre>

Q: How to minify php page html output? A: <h1>An alternative solution with ob_start() and ob_get_clean()</h1>
<p><sub>This answer focuses more on how to produce the output. It's not something I've used. But I think it should be said here to complement my post about the Minifier class.</sub></p>
<p>If you want to, you can separate your php files from the minifying process by putting all your files under a protected path. Then, for each such file, create a public dummy-file with the same name. Like so...</p>
<pre><code>/
    a.php ← dummy
    b.php ← dummy
    c.php ← dummy
    /your protected path/
        a.php ← web page
        b.php ← web page
        c.php ← web page
</code></pre>
<p>Then, each of your dummy files could contain the same code as follows.</p>
<pre><code>&lt;?php
require_once('/path to classes/Minifier.php');
ob_start();
include __DIR__ . '/your protected path/' . basename(__FILE__);
$html = ob_get_clean();
$minifier = new Minifier();
echo $minifier-&gt;minifyHTML($html);
?&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>The interesting part here is, of course, the <code>include</code> line. That's where your pages are made. Calling <strong>/a.php</strong> will result in a minified reply. Calling <strong>/your protected path/a.php</strong> will result in the same web page. But without compression, which is good during testing.</p>
